      "headline": "The Kickstart Question: What Is on Your Mind?",
      "keyInsight": "Most meetings begin with vague weather chatter or meandering status reports that waste precious minutes. People often talk about trivial details while dodging the real elephant in the room. When you open with \"What is on your mind?\", you cut through the weeds like a sharp kitchen knife. It is open enough to let them pick the topic, yet focused enough to demand immediate honesty. You uncover what is truly buzzing inside their thoughts rather than guessing their agenda from five miles away.",
      "content": "Imagine walking into a workshop cluttered with piles of lumber, random nails, and half-finished projects. If you ask someone to \"give an update,\" they will recite an endless list of every rusty tool on the bench. You waste twenty minutes and learn nothing meaningful about the real roadblock.\n\nNow try asking, \"What is on your mind?\" This question works like turning on a bright spotlight in that dusty garage. It gives the speaker permission to bypass polite small talk and point straight at the broken gear. They choose what matters most to them right now.\n\nTo make this question even sharper, keep the 3P model in your back pocket: Projects, People, and Patterns. Is the issue a stuck project, a strained relationship with a coworker, or a bad personal pattern? This simple lens turns messy, confusing chatter into neat, actionable clarity.",
